<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hosni Mubarak</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1928">1928</a>–<a href="/years/2020">2020</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Muhammad Hosni Mubarak</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hosni Mubarak was an Egyptian air force commander who became vice-president and, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anwar-sadat">Anwar Sadat</a>'s assassination in 1981, president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. He kept the peace with Israel, relied on emergency laws and close ties with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, and ruled for almost thirty years. Mass protests forced him from office in February 2011.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hosni-mubarak.jpg" alt="Hosni Mubarak" width="320" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hosni_Mubarak_-_Official_Photo_(cropped).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Hosni Mubarak was born on 4 May 1928 in Kafr El-Meselha, in the Monufia Governorate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. On 2 February 1949 he left the Military Academy for the Air Force Academy and was commissioned as a pilot officer on 13 March 1950. He served in various units, including two years in a Spitfire fighter squadron, and returned to the Air Force Academy as an instructor until early 1959. From February 1959 to June 1961 he trained in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, at a pilot school in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> and at Kant Air Base near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishkek">Bishkek</a>. He commanded the Egyptian Air Force from 1972 to 1975 and became air chief marshal in 1973.</p><p>In April 1975 President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anwar-sadat">Anwar Sadat</a> appointed Mubarak vice president. In September of that year he travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riyadh">Riyadh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> to win Saudi and Syrian acceptance of the Sinai II disengagement agreement with Israel; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>'s president <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hafez-al-assad">Hafez al-Assad</a> refused to meet him, but in Saudi Arabia he formed a friendship with Crown Prince Fahd. Mubarak was injured when soldiers led by Lieutenant Khalid Islambouli assassinated Sadat in October 1981, and he became the fourth president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in a single-candidate referendum. He renewed his term by single-candidate referendums in 1987, 1993 and 1999.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Mubarak upheld the Camp David Accords with Israel, though Israeli journalists wrote of a cold peace. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> had been suspended from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arab-league">Arab League</a> because of the treaty; Mubarak met King Fahd of Saudi Arabia in 1982, and in 1989 Egypt's membership was restored and the League's headquarters returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>. Egypt joined the coalition in the 1991 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-war">Gulf War</a>, and afterwards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, the Gulf states and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> forgave about 20 billion dollars of its debt. In 2005, under United States pressure, he held the country's first multi-party election and won it; observers said it was marred by rigging.</p><p>The state of emergency in force since the 1967 war was never lifted. Human rights organisations reported arbitrary detention and the systematic use of torture by the security services, and corruption was widespread.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Protests began in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> and other cities on 25 January 2011. On 1 February Mubarak announced he would not contest the election due in September, but the demonstrations continued, and on 11 February Vice President Omar Suleiman announced his resignation and the transfer of power to the military. Mubarak was tried on charges of killing protesters, sentenced to life imprisonment in 2012, retried after an appeal, and acquitted by the Court of Cassation on 2 March 2017. He died on 25 February 2020 and was given a military funeral.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hosni Mubarak” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Hosni Mubarak</b>, confirmed in 1981 as sole candidate, governed for thirty years through the National Democratic Party and laws of 1993, 1995 and 1999 curbing syndicates, the press and NGOs; Human Rights Watch and Amnesty International documented torture and Egypt's use as a 'torture hub' in the war…</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-egypt">Republic of Egypt · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In Egypt protests began on 25 January; the government tried to cut off the Internet, and on 11 February <b>Hosni Mubarak</b> resigned after 18 days, handing power to the armed forces.</blockquote><a href="/movement/arab-spring">Arab Spring · Course of Events</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Kafr el-Muṣīlḥa, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. Died in Cairo, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=30.54,31.02&amp;year=1987" aria-label="Kafr el-Muṣīlḥa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5354 1050 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Hosni Mubarak"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5804 1320h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5804 1320h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=30.54,31.02&amp;year=1987">What was here in 1987? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
